  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Narada, a great sage, asked Vishnu, the preserver, what Maya is. Vishnu did not explain. He asked for water. Narada never brought it, and that was the answer. Narada had come to Vaikuntha, Vishnu's home, to tell him how his curse had come true on his father, Brahma. Vishnu smiled and said it was all Maya. Narada asked what that meant. Vishnu said he was thirsty. He told Narada to fetch some water, and then he would answer. Narada walked to the river outside. By the bank, he saw a young woman. She was so lovely that he forgot Vishnu, his errand, and his thirst. He married her right there and built a house on the sand by the river. Years passed. Narada became a father, then a grandfather. The house had become his whole world. Then one day, the sky turned gray. Thunder roared, rain poured, and the river flooded. The water swept his family away. Suddenly, Narada was back in Vaikuntha, standing before Vishnu. Vishnu asked, \"Where is the water? I told you I am thirsty.\" Narada stood stunned. His wife, his children, his home, none of it had been real. Vishnu said that was Maya, attachment to a world that does not last. It makes us chase what slips away like sand. Then he smiled and warned Narada that there would be much more Maya to come.",
      "start_time": 0.16,
      "end_time": 82.58,
      "speaker": {
        "id": "speaker_0",
